{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1793662409116474001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"unwhite-worrying-bluebottle","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":3,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"925e4a3c31ca3cce70d5971ec4269e2c1ed92df5fa22ff1ca3fddc0392ea2081","event_uuid":"bb52a961-e9b4-4d37-be01-ca6e23a575cf","position":{"commit_sha":"8670e151f8b8de524d98617a4b12b75d509934f8","position":"1793647980812182766-bb52a961-e9b4-4d37-be01-ca6e23a575cf"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"a416b413e8b3843ba8a5f0b264b7e25fbc3b091ee0d08ee89baa849ae8b203c3","event_uuid":"bd30fd81-42ee-4bca-a236-57bee36bc1d9","position":{"commit_sha":"d03da2b30f8e718ebddeb4a7d731c24d7b023944","position":"1793648100768709578-bd30fd81-42ee-4bca-a236-57bee36bc1d9"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"f5570d5f06110e95351d46a0713b5955363d9655adb0b7621b9b73669622807f","event_uuid":"405c07a9-ce09-4004-93ed-9f1645975664","position":{"commit_sha":"b538f43dcd8b57046c9e654467da034e07cda1c2","position":"1793648254269123370-405c07a9-ce09-4004-93ed-9f1645975664"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## Correction: the `tests` criterion is BLOCKING, and already covers 3 of the 5 proposed shapes\n\nMeasured at `origin/main` `4cf1ae42946`. This ticket's description and the parent epic's\nFinding 3 both rest on a premise that is no longer true. Recording the measurement before\nthe scope is settled, so the ticket is not reviewed against a false baseline.\n\n**1. It is blocking, not advisory.** `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json`:\n\n```\n\"tests\": {\n  \"default_posture\": \"blocking\",\n  \"block_threshold\": 0.54,\n  \"blocking_enabled\": true\n}\n```\n\nThe `_comment` on that entry records why: bug `obese-dihedral-ermine` flipped it to\nblocking from the code-v4 replay, \"blocks 99 changes / 8.0%, operator-accepted.\" The\nepic's text was written 2026-09-07 and predates the flip.\n\n**This ticket's acceptance criterion \"The criterion's posture is unchanged by this story.\nVerified by a test asserting it is still advisory\" is therefore FALSE as written and will\nbe removed.**\n\n**2. Three of the 5 proposed shapes are already in the rubric**\n(`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`):\n\n| Proposed shape | Already covered by |\n|---|---|\n| pinning refactor mechanics | `change-detector` — litmus names \"a private method extracted / the module reorganized, WITHOUT any observable-behavior change\" |\n| pinning re-export identity | `change-detector` — \"asserts an internal name, structure, or intermediate state\" |\n| pinning import shape | `change-detector`, same clause |\n\nPlus `source-grepping` and `existence-only` are already flagged, and `existence-only` is\ndescribed in the rubric as \"a change-detector that breaks on a rename.\"\n\n**3. What is genuinely absent.** Searched the whole 130-line rubric for `expir`, `LOC`,\n`line count`, `size cap`, `seam` — the only hits are unrelated words.\n\n- **LOC floor/ceiling pinning** appears nowhere. A test asserting a file stays under N\n  lines is not described by any antipattern.\n- **Re-running a gate script over the real tree** is currently **VALID**, not flagged.\n  Guard (iii) permits greps of command output \"provided the producer's liveness is\n  established\", and the Rule-5 structural-artifact exception explicitly validates a\n  `grep`/`awk` over a non-executable instruction artifact. The epic's complaint about\n  \"around 30 modules re-running lint scripts over the real tree\" is therefore a request to\n  flag something the rubric currently blesses.\n- **Expiry** exists, but only for one shape. Guard (viii) accepts \"a strict xfail\n  (`strict=True`) whose `reason=` (or an adjacent comment/linked ticket) names a concrete\n  removal condition.\" The concept is present; it is simply not available to a\n  mechanics-pinning test.\n\n**Consequence for scope.** Writing all 5 shapes as proposed would restate 3 antipatterns\nthe rubric already carries. Adding duplicate rubric text inside an epic whose purpose is\nreducing complexity would be self-defeating, and `A1`'s NIH facet exists to catch exactly\nthat. The scope decision is with the operator.","timestamp":1793648254269123370}],"created_at":1793647980812182766,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"bd30fd81-42ee-4bca-a236-57bee36bc1d9","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"d278-3792-6538-4c8e"}],"description":"## Parent\n\nEpic `77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec`, authorized at full scope by the operator on 2026-11-02.\nThis is deliverable 4.\n\n## Independent of the net-mechanism and sunset stories\n\nThose two edit the plan-review criteria registry. This one edits the CODE-REVIEW `tests`\ncriterion. Different gate, different rubric, no shared file — so it can run in parallel\nwith them.\n\n## The gap\n\nNo gate sees tests as complexity. The `tests` criterion on the code-review gate is\nADVISORY, and nothing anywhere asks whether a test is pinning BEHAVIOUR or merely pinning\nthe shape of a refactor.\n\nThe result is measurable in the tree the program just cleaned. Gate-induced tests the\ncomplexity-reduction program deleted included: four seam-guard modules written by\nmodule-size-cap split commits (2026-07-30, 08-13, 08-19); 88 held-out twins left behind by\nthe rebar-implement convention because it had no merge step; around 30 modules that\nre-ran lint scripts over the real tree; and CI-parity snapshot tests.\n\nEach of those was added in good faith to satisfy a gate. None of them tested a behaviour a\nuser could observe. They are a maintenance surface that grows every time a gate fires.\n\n## The change\n\nA test taxonomy, attached to the code-review `tests` criterion. Five shapes are named as\nNOT behaviour tests:\n\n1. pinning refactor mechanics (that a split happened, that a helper exists)\n2. pinning re-export identity (that `a.b` is the same object as `c.d`)\n3. pinning a LOC floor or ceiling on a file\n4. pinning import shape (that a module imports a particular way)\n5. re-running a gate script over the real tree\n\nA test of one of these shapes either carries an expiry, or it does not land.\n\nThe taxonomy describes shapes, not file names, so it does not need updating when files\nmove. It names the expiry as the escape hatch rather than banning the shapes outright,\nbecause some of them are legitimately useful for a short window during a migration.\n\n## Scope boundary\n\nThis story changes what the `tests` criterion ASKS. It does not change the criterion's\nposture from advisory to blocking — that is a separate judgement with its own blast\nradius, and bundling it here would make the red proofs ambiguous about which change did\nthe work.\n\n## Provider neutrality\n\nrebar must support a wide variety of providers and models. The taxonomy is rubric text and\nmust not assume a single vendor's model or tool-calling syntax.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The `tests` criterion's rubric names all 5 non-behaviour test shapes. Verified by\n      reading the rubric and by a test asserting each shape's name is present.\n- [ ] A change adding a test of each of the 5 shapes, with no expiry, produces a finding\n      from this criterion — one case per shape, 5 cases. Verified by tests.\n- [ ] A change adding a test of one of those shapes WITH an expiry produces no finding.\n      Verified by a test.\n- [ ] A change adding an ordinary behaviour test produces no finding. Verified by a test,\n      so the taxonomy is proven not to fire on normal work.\n- [ ] RED PROOF: one of the 4 named seam-guard modules, replayed as a change, produces a\n      finding. Verified by a test.\n- [ ] The criterion's posture is unchanged by this story. Verified by a test asserting it\n      is still advisory.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes.\n\n## Plan grounding\n\nExact file:line citations for the `tests` criterion rubric, its routing entry, and the\ncode-review criteria overlay are being measured against `origin/main` and will be written\ninto this description before plan review is requested. This ticket is NOT ready for review\nuntil that is done.","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[["parent","77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec"],["relates_to","d278-3792-6538-4c8e"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec","parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_id":"f86e-b57b-008d-4652","ticket_type":"story","title":"Add a test taxonomy to the code-review tests criterion: mechanics-pinning tests carry an expiry or do not land","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["bb52a961-e9b4-4d37-be01-ca6e23a575cf","bd30fd81-42ee-4bca-a236-57bee36bc1d9","405c07a9-ce09-4004-93ed-9f1645975664"]},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1793662409116474001,"uuid":"7223f4c6-266f-4dc1-86bd-670ac792e695"}